1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A relation from A to B is any set of arrows. A function is the special case where every element of A has exactly one arrow.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
With two elements in A and three in B there are six possible arrows, so 64 relations — and only 9 of them are functions.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Promise one: total. No element of the domain is left without an image.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Promise two: single-valued. No element of the domain gets two images. A relation missing either promise is not a function.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
f from A to B. A is the domain, B is the codomain, and the set of values actually taken is the range.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Here the range is a and c, smaller than the codomain a, b, c, d. The two are equal exactly when f is onto.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The codomain is a promise about where values are allowed to live; the range is a fact about where they landed.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Every element of the domain has an image, so the range is never empty when the domain is not.

9
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Injective, or one-to-one, means no two different elements of the domain share an image.

10
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Read the matrix down each column: at most one 1 per column. Here column a has two, so f is not injective.

11
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The witness is a pair: f(1) equals f(2) equals a with 1 different from 2. One colliding pair is enough.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Consequence: an injection needs at least as many elements in B as in A. If m exceeds n, the number of injections is zero.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Surjective, or onto, means every element of the codomain is hit at least once.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Read the matrix down each column again: at least one 1 per column. Here column c is empty, so f is not onto.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Consequence: an onto function needs at least as many domain elements as codomain elements.

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Counting onto functions needs inclusion and exclusion: 3 to the 4 minus 3 times 2 to the 4 plus 3 times 1 to the 4, which is 81 minus 48 plus 3, that is 36.

17
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Bijective means injective and surjective at once: every codomain element is hit exactly once.

18
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:18,000
On finite sets of the same size the three properties collapse: injective, surjective and bijective are the same condition.

19
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Of the 27 functions from a three-element set to itself, 6 are injective, 6 are surjective, and they are the same 6.

20
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The collapse needs finiteness. On the natural numbers, n maps to n plus 1 is injective and not surjective.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Four counts, one per row: all functions, injections, surjections, bijections.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
All functions: n to the m, because each of the m domain elements picks one of n targets independently.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Injections: n falling m, that is n factorial over n minus m factorial, and zero as soon as m exceeds n. Bijections: n factorial when m equals n.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Surjections have no product formula. Inclusion and exclusion is the method, and this page checks every figure by enumeration.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Compose g from A to B with f from B to C. The composite f after g sends a to f of g of a, and the order matters.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Injective after injective is injective, and surjective after surjective is surjective.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Only one direction survives. If the composite is injective then the inner function g is injective, but the outer function f need not be.

28
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:34,000
If the composite is onto then the outer function f is onto, but the inner function g need not be, even when f is also onto. That is the shape of the GATE 2025 question.

29
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:42,000
An inverse function exists exactly when f is bijective. Then f inverse after f is the identity on A, and f after f inverse is the identity on B.

30
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:50,000
The preimage of a set is always defined, bijection or not. The inverse function is the stricter object.

31
00:03:50,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Squaring on the reals is not injective, so it has no inverse on the reals. Restricted to the non-negative reals it becomes a bijection and gains one.

32
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
For subsets S and T of the domain, the image of the union is the union of the images. Always.

33
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
For intersections only one inclusion survives: the image of the intersection sits inside the intersection of the images.

34
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Here 1 and 3 both map to p, so the image of the intersection is the single element q while the intersection of the images is p and q. Equality holds exactly when f is injective.

35
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Preimages are better behaved: the preimage of an intersection is the intersection of the preimages, for every function. That is GATE 2014 Set 3.

36
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:40,000
One: total and single-valued. Check the domain first, because a relation that misses an element is not a function.

37
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Two: read the matrix by columns. Two ones in a column kills injectivity, and an empty column kills surjectivity.

38
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Three: on finite sets of equal size, injective, surjective and bijective are one property. Without that hypothesis they are three.

39
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Four: images commute with union only, while preimages commute with union, intersection and complement.

